    "body": "The submitted explicit nonboundary theorem is mathematically valid. Three independent adversarial reviews and ROOT's all-index reconstruction verified the source, singleton-stopping cost, fixed square-position rank, triangular order-statistic limit, uniform error bounds, and failure under every deterministic real centering sequence. Both finite verification programs were reproduced; their Python assertion-removal weakness was repaired and deliberate false variants were rejected in ordinary and optimized modes.\n\nThe original unrestricted marginal-convergence question nevertheless already had a negative answer. Leckey\u2013Neininger\u2013Sulzbach, arXiv:1605.02352v2, Proposition 2.9, explicitly gives non-tight fixed boundary marginals; Proposition 2.5's two-atom limit also implies failure under every deterministic center. In this exact source an earlier boundary example is t=3/8, with normalized costs 9/4 and 7/4 and equal limiting masses. These full primary theorem/proof passages, including the accepted manuscript, were inspected. The final publisher typeset body remains unavailable. See https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.02352v2 and https://doi.org/10.1016/j.spa.2018.03.009.\n\nThis priority finding does **not** establish that the PR's precise continuity-rank construction is already known. Its changing-depth jump-height/distance geometry adds verified content; none of the three bounded priority audits located an earlier theorem or application identifying this explicit rank. Historical novelty and current global open status of that refinement remain unestablished. The 2015 thesis \u00a76.1 actually asks about continuity's role, and this construction supplies a partial negative instance, but it does not classify continuity ranks or determine almost-everywhere behavior: https://d-nb.info/107268702X/34.\n\nReasonable repair preserves the exact proof, fixes the supporting checks, credits the known boundary obstruction, and limits the claim to a continuity-rank refinement. Reframing it as a first novel resolution of the original unrestricted question would still be inaccurate. Under the current programme's requirement to publish a decisive novel resolution of the original problem, this original claimed-solved submission is therefore closed without merging or creating a first-resolution preprint/DOI/tracker row. The original-target QUEUE outcome is already_solved; the explicit refinement is retained with its verified mathematics and unresolved priority. This is a priority/scope disposition, not a claim that the theorem is false or devoid of additional value.\n\nThe full mathematical and priority findings are preserved in draft_pr_publication_program_20260930/audits/pr166_30002720. A separate Baire-category consequence assembled during this audit is recorded with its own chronology and fresh mathematical review, rather than attributed to an earlier publication or asserted to identify the square rank. No almost-everywhere conclusion is claimed.\n\n",
